<%# The page that decides whether a non-technical visitor believes the rest
    of the site. Its job is to make "run your own model" concrete, and to be
    straight about where a home model is worse — per ABOUT_PLAN §6, honesty
    is the strategy here, not a disclaimer at the bottom. %>

<section class="mt-12 max-w-3xl space-y-5">
  <p>
    Every time this app writes something — a letter, a plan, an explanation
    of your lab results — a language model does that writing. This page is
    about where that model lives, because it is the difference between an
    assistant you own and a subscription you rent.
  </p>
  <p>
    It can run on your computer, and we think it should. You install one of
    several free programs that serve a model locally, point this app at it once in Settings, and from
    then on the writing happens on your hardware, in your house, with no
    account and no per-word charge.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="mt-14">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">How it fits together</h2>
  <div class="mt-6 grid grid-cols-1 gap-4 sm:grid-cols-3">
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "1 · A model runner") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        A small free program that loads a model file and answers questions
        on your machine. Ollama is the usual first choice because it
        installs in one step; LM Studio gives you a friendlier window;
        llama.cpp is the lean option underneath both.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "2 · A model") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        A file you download once, a few gigabytes, and keep. There are
        dozens of good open ones and new ones every few months. You are not
        locked to whichever you pick first — swapping is changing a name in
        a box.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "3 · This app") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        In Settings, you tell it the address the runner is listening on and
        the name of the model. That's the whole integration. Nothing else in
        the app changes when you swap models later.
      </p>
    <% end %>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">What your computer needs</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    Less than people expect, and more than a phone. The rough shape:
  </p>
  <ul class="mt-6 space-y-4">
    <li class="card p-5">
      <h3 class="font-semibold">A recent laptop or desktop with 16GB of memory</h3>
      <p class="mt-1.5 text-sm text-soft">
        Runs a small model comfortably. Good enough for the letters, the
        summaries, the plain-language explanations, and the daily filing —
        which is most of what the app asks a model to do.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li class="card p-5">
      <h3 class="font-semibold">32GB, or a machine with a real graphics card</h3>
      <p class="mt-1.5 text-sm text-soft">
        Runs a mid-sized model, and answers arrive at reading speed rather
        than at typing speed. This is the sweet spot, and a second-hand
        desktop that hits it costs less than a year of most subscriptions.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li class="card p-5">
      <h3 class="font-semibold">A Mac with Apple silicon</h3>
      <p class="mt-1.5 text-sm text-soft">
        Punches above its price here, because the graphics chip can use the
        machine's whole memory. An M-series Mac with 32GB is one of the
        easiest good setups there is.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p class="mt-6 text-soft">
    The app itself is undemanding — it is a small web application and a
    database file, and will run happily on a mini PC or a home server. It is
    only the model that wants the memory, and the two do not have to be the
    same machine: plenty of households run the app on something tiny and
    point it at the desktop upstairs.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">Where a home model is worse</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    Straight answer: a model you run at home is behind the largest hosted
    ones, and anyone who tells you otherwise is selling something. What
    matters is that the gap is uneven, and this app leans on the half where
    it is small.
  </p>
  <div class="mt-6 grid grid-cols-1 gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "Happy on a small model") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        Summarising a document you supplied. Turning a letter into plain
        language. Drafting a formal letter to a template. Filing things into
        the right places. Breaking a goal into steps. This is the bulk of
        the app, and it is mostly comprehension and structure rather than
        knowledge.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "Wants a bigger one") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        Long open-ended conversations that hold a lot at once — the tutor
        across a whole subject, a mock interview that keeps its thread, an
        argument about a complicated document. These work on a small model
        and get noticeably better on a large one.
      </p>
    <% end %>
  </div>
  <p class="mt-6 text-soft">
    There is also a whole half of the app that never asks a model anything:
    the calendar, the documents, the tasks, the photos, the reader, the
    vault. Those work identically on a machine with no model configured at
    all, and they are deliberately the foundation rather than the garnish.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">What about the big hosted models?</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    The app can use them — OpenAI, Anthropic and Google are in the list in
    Settings, because a household without a spare computer should still get
    the help, and refusing would be us making that choice for you. But a
    hosted model makes this a nicer front end for somebody else's
    subscription, and turns everything on the
    <%= link_to "privacy page", about_page_path("privacy"), class: "text-primary-700 hover:underline" %>
    into a promise about their behaviour rather than a fact about yours.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    So the settings page makes the case for running your own before it
    offers anything else, and one switch takes the hosted ones off the list
    for the whole household. The whole argument of this project is that the
    thing doing the reading should be a thing you own.
  </p>
</section>
